WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.205
Here is a function that adds up amounts.

2
00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:08.912
It takes a MutableList of Number, and it only reads from it.

3
00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.945
You have cents, a MutableList&lt;Int&gt;.

4
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.507
An Int is a Number.

5
00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:15.892
So does total(cents) compile?

6
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:17.795
No.

7
00:00:17.795 --> 00:00:21.984
A MutableList&lt;Int&gt; is not a MutableList&lt;Number&gt;.

8
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.334
A mutable list has two doors.

9
00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:26.174
get hands an element out.

10
00:00:26.174 --> 00:00:27.425
add takes one in.

11
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:34.431
If cents could pass as a list of Number, total could push 2.5 through the add door, into a list of ints.

12
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:39.153
The element type sits at both doors, so MutableList stays invariant.

13
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:41.468
But total never uses the add door.

14
00:00:41.468 --> 00:00:46.429
So it asks for a view of the list with that door shut: MutableList&lt;out Number&gt;.

15
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.968
Read it as: a list of some subtype of Number, and I don't know which.

16
00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:54.272
Now cents fits, and so does rates, a list of Doubles.

17
00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:56.329
Every read comes out as a Number.

18
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:58.973
Now total calls amounts.add(0).

19
00:00:58.973 --> 00:01:01.886
Zero is an Int, and cents holds ints.

20
00:01:01.886 --> 00:01:03.145
Does it compile?

21
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:05.592
No.

22
00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:09.431
Through the view, the compiler can't tell which list it has.

23
00:01:09.431 --> 00:01:12.247
It could be rates, and zero is not a Double.

24
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.822
javac calls that unknown element type CAP#1.

25
00:01:15.822 --> 00:01:19.920
Nothing you can write is known to fit it, so nothing goes in.

26
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:23.364
Calls that take no element, like clear, still work.

27
00:01:23.364 --> 00:01:25.627
The view limits types, not changes.

28
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.713
Now flip it.

29
00:01:26.713 --> 00:01:28.918
padWithZeros only adds zeros.

30
00:01:28.918 --> 00:01:32.796
So it asks for the other view: MutableList&lt;in Int&gt;.

31
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:35.464
A list of Int, of Number or of Any.

32
00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:38.429
Each one takes an Int, so add(0) is safe.

33
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.423
Reading is the side that gets weaker.

34
00:01:41.423 --> 00:01:48.157
The first element might be "EUR", so a read comes back typed only as Any?, which fits anything.

35
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:52.919
Writing out or in on one use of a type is called use-site variance.

36
00:01:52.919 --> 00:01:55.451
The view itself is a type projection.

37
00:01:56.333 --> 00:01:59.104
Java has only this kind of variance.

38
00:01:59.104 --> 00:02:03.875
Its ? extends is the read view, and ? super is the write view.

39
00:02:03.875 --> 00:02:08.108
Kotlin's out and in compile to exactly these wildcards.

40
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:09.747
The rule.

41
00:02:09.747 --> 00:02:13.993
A type that reads and writes T stays invariant.

42
00:02:13.993 --> 00:02:16.975
Only reads it: out, or ? extends.

43
00:02:16.975 --> 00:02:19.776
Only writes it: in, or ? super.

44
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:20.759
Quick check.

45
00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:25.438
copyInto reads from one mutable list, and adds each item to another.

46
00:02:25.438 --> 00:02:28.465
Which parameter gets out, and which gets in?

47
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.564
The from list gets out.

48
00:02:32.564 --> 00:02:34.099
The to list gets in.
